# skynet.env.example — template for /opt/skynet/skynet.env
#
# Copy this file to /opt/skynet/skynet.env on the deployment host and fill in
# the values before running `docker compose up`. This file is NOT a secret —
# it documents the required env vars. The real skynet.env contains actual
# credentials and is never committed to git.

# ─── Core ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Public HTTPS URL of this Skynet instance (no trailing slash).
# Used by the distributor to write ~/fleet/host/parent on managed hosts.
SKYNET_PUBLIC_URL=https://your-instance.example.com

# Home directory mount source: the host-side path bind-mounted into the
# container at /host-home. Required for co-located deployments.
SKYNET_HOME_MOUNT_SRC=/home/ubuntu

# Where this instance's source code is published, shown in Preferences →
# About. Skynet builds include AGPL-3.0 code (SuperDoc, the Word editor), so
# if you run a modified Skynet, point this at your public fork or branch
# containing exactly what you deploy. Unmodified instances can leave it unset.
# SKYNET_SOURCE_URL=https://github.com/your-org/skynet

# Document converter sidecar (docker-compose service `converter`): opens
# .doc/.odt in the Word editor, .xls/.ods in the Excel viewer and
# .ppt/.pptx/.odp as PDFs. docker-compose.yml sets this for you; leave it
# unset (or drop the service) to turn conversion off — those files then get
# the download card instead.
# SKYNET_CONVERTER_URL=http://converter:3030

# ─── Voice: speech-to-text provider ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Which service transcribes voice input (POST /voice/transcribe) and audio
# agents send to the stt service. Unset means `bedrock` — Amazon Nova 2 Sonic
# via the instance's IAM role, audio stays in AWS. Use that on any instance whose audio must stay under an AWS BAA.
#
# The other providers send audio to a third party, are much faster (whole
# clip in one request, ~1 s back) and more accurate. All are pay-as-you-go;
# a typical heavy dictation month (~7 h of audio) costs a few dollars or less.
#
#   elevenlabs  ElevenLabs Scribe v2 — most accurate.      ELEVENLABS_API_KEY
#   mistral     Mistral Voxtral Mini Transcribe — close     MISTRAL_API_KEY
#               second, cheaper. (Free "Experiment" tier
#               may train on your audio — opt out in the
#               Mistral console, or use a paid key.)
#   groq        Whisper Large v3 Turbo on Groq — fastest,  GROQ_API_KEY
#               near-free, a step down in accuracy.
#
# An unknown value or a missing key makes /voice/transcribe answer 503; it
# never falls back to a different provider.
# STT_PROVIDER=elevenlabs
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY=
#
# Optional: override the provider's default model id (scribe_v2,
# voxtral-mini-latest, whisper-large-v3-turbo) and/or pin the spoken language
# (ISO-639-1, e.g. "en") instead of auto-detecting it.
# STT_MODEL=
# STT_LANGUAGE=en

# ─── Voice: text-to-speech provider ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Which service speaks agent messages (speak buttons, hands-free voice mode,
# voice previews). Unset means `polly` — Amazon Polly's generative engine via
# the instance's IAM role, text stays in AWS. Use that on any instance whose
# content must stay under an AWS BAA.
#
# The other providers send message text to a third party. List prices
# (2026-10) per million characters spoken; a heavy listener hears ~3M/month:
#
#   polly       (default) ~$30. Its seven generative voices.
#   openai      OpenAI speech, ~$15 (gpt-4o-mini-tts).   OPENAI_API_KEY
#               Its fixed set of named voices.
#   elevenlabs  ElevenLabs, ~$80 (multilingual v2;        ELEVENLABS_API_KEY
#               ~$40 on the flash/turbo models). The
#               account's own voices, fetched live.
#
# The voice pickers in the app show the active provider's voices. Voices
# already saved on identities, roles and users are never rewritten: a saved
# voice the active provider doesn't offer is skipped in favour of the next one
# (identity → role → the user's fallback voice → the provider default).
#
# An unknown value or a missing / rejected key makes speaking answer 503; it
# never falls back to a different provider. OPENAI_API_KEY and
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY are shared with the agent services / STT settings.
# An ElevenLabs key restricted to speech-to-text won't do: it also needs the
# text-to-speech and voices (read) permissions.
# TTS_PROVIDER=openai
#
# Optional: override the provider's default model id (gpt-4o-mini-tts,
# eleven_multilingual_v2; ignored by polly) and/or the voice used when no
# saved voice is available (defaults: Joanna, marin, River =
# SAz9YHcvj6GT2YYXdXww — ElevenLabs takes the voice CODE, not its name).
# Both belong to one provider: change or clear them when TTS_PROVIDER changes.
# A default voice the active provider doesn't have makes speaking answer 503.
# TTS_MODEL=
# TTS_DEFAULT_VOICE=

# ─── Agent services (backend holds the keys; agents never see them) ─────────
#
# Each of these is optional. Leave one unset and that service answers
# agents with "not_configured" instead of calling the provider. Services are
# defined under src/backend/agent-services/ (see its README to add one).

# agent-phone — Twilio account used to place voice calls for agents. All
# three are required; without them every call returns "not_configured".
# TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER is a voice-capable number bought on that account (E.164);
# every call comes from it. The callee's reply is transcribed with the
# instance's STT provider (STT_PROVIDER above). Users also need a phone number
# on file; an admin turns the feature on per user by setting one in the admin
# user modal.
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=<AC...>
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=<your-twilio-auth-token>
# TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=+1XXXXXXXXXX

# image-gen and identity avatar generation — OpenAI API key (gpt-image-1).
# Without it, image-gen requests fail with reason "not_configured" and avatar
# generation is unavailable.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=<your-openai-api-key>

# image-gen fleet-wide rate limit, in requests per minute. Defaults to 30.
# SKYNET_IMAGE_GEN_RPM=30

# stt (speech to text for agents) — uses the voice-input provider configured
# above (STT_PROVIDER, its API key, STT_MODEL, STT_LANGUAGE); nothing extra to
# set. With the default bedrock provider, agent audio stays in AWS too.
# Fleet-wide rate limit, in requests per minute. Defaults to 30.
# SKYNET_STT_RPM=30

# skynet-hostd — send each long-lived SSH connection's routine commands through
# one helper channel (~/.local/bin/skynet-hostd, installed by the distributor)
# instead of one exec channel per command. Over Tailscale SSH every exec
# channel is a fresh login on the host, so this turns thousands of logins an
# hour into one per connection. "*" = every host; otherwise a comma list of
# host IPs (e.g. 100.113.23.63). Unset = off. Hosts without the helper fall
# back to plain execs automatically.
# SKYNET_HOSTD_PEERS=*
